To create a responsive grid layout using CSS Grid, you use the `grid-template-columns` property with responsive units like `fr`, `auto`, or `minmax()`, combined with media queries or the `auto-fit`/`auto-fill` keywords:

```css
/* Basic responsive grid with auto-fit */
.grid-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
  gap: 20px;
  padding: 20px;
}

/* With media queries for more control */
.grid-container {
  display: grid;
  gap: 20px;
  padding: 20px;
}

/* Mobile: single column */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .grid-container {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}

/* Tablet: two columns */
@media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1024px) {
  .grid-container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
}

/* Desktop: three columns */
@media (min-width: 1025px) {
  .grid-container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
}

/* Grid item styling */
.grid-item {
  background: #f0f0f0;
  padding: 20px;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
```

The `auto-fit` keyword automatically adjusts the number of columns based on available space. The `minmax(250px, 1fr)` function sets a minimum column width of 250px and a maximum of 1 fraction unit. You can also use `grid-template-areas` for complex layouts and `grid-column`/`grid-row` to span items across multiple columns. CSS Grid is perfect for two-dimensional layouts (rows and columns), while Flexbox is better for one-dimensional layouts.

For a navigation bar specifically with CSS Grid, use: `grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;` for the main navbar layout with logo and links, and media queries to stack vertically on mobile.
